---
import Hale from './Hale.astro';
import SystemMap from './SystemMap.astro';
import StatusRail from './StatusRail.astro';
// Inlined rather than <img>: only inline SVG can be re-inked by the page's
// tokens. The files stay byte-for-byte `hale topology graph` output; the
// renderer's own <style> block and fixed size are dropped here, at render.
import busRaw from '../diagrams/model-bus-graph.svg?raw';
import residueRaw from '../diagrams/model-residue-graph.svg?raw';
const inline = (svg: string) => svg.replace(/<style>[\s\S]*?<\/style>\n?/, '').replace(/ width="\d+" height="\d+"/, '');
const busGraph = inline(busRaw);
const residueGraph = inline(residueRaw);

/*
  Offstage context for the panels below (scripts/check-site.mjs prepends
  `_prelude` to every snippet in this file, and `main_prelude` to `main`,
  before `hale check`; nothing renders them).
*/
const _prelude = `type Sample { id: Int; value: Int; }
`;

const bus = `topic Reading { payload: Sample; }

locus Ingest {
    bus { publish Reading; }

    fn received(s: Sample) {
        Reading <- s;
    }
}

locus Rollup {
    bus { subscribe Reading as add; }

    fn add(s: Sample) {
        // owns the rolling state
    }
}`;

const main_prelude = `topic Reading { payload: Sample; }

locus Ingest {
    bus { publish Reading; }
    fn received(s: Sample) { Reading <- s; }
}

locus Rollup {
    bus { subscribe Reading as add; }
    fn add(s: Sample) { }
}
`;
const main = `main locus App {
    params {
        ingest: Ingest = Ingest { };
        rollup: Rollup = Rollup { };
    }

    placement {
        ingest: pinned(core = 1);
        rollup: cooperative(pool = compute);
    }

    bindings {
        Reading: unix("/run/readings.sock", role: listen);
    }
}`;
---
<div class="page-head">
    <h1>What a Hale program describes</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A running system: who owns which state, what
      talks to what, where each part runs, and what may influence what.
      Those are usually written in different languages. Hale makes them
      views of one program.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>A type is data. A locus is anything that lives.</h2>
    <p>
      A <code>type</code> is shape. A <code>locus</code> is anything that owns
      state or takes part in flow: an application, a service, a connection,
      a worker, a cache, a pipeline stage, an adapter. Anyone who knows actors, or services that only talk over queues, knows most of it already;
      the rest of this page is what the compiler can do once nothing else
      exists. Each locus has a
      parent, may own children, and owns a memory region. The ownership tree
      is therefore also the lifetime tree and the failure tree. A locus that dissolves takes what it owns with it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <SystemMap />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Three graphs, and the compiler knows how they meet</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid4" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr))">
    <article><b>Ownership</b><p>Who owns state, lifetime, children and recovery.</p></article>
    <article><b>Communication</b><p>Which typed topics connect publishers to subscribers.</p></article>
    <article><b>Placement</b><p>Where each locus runs and how each edge is carried.</p></article>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p style="margin-top:var(--s6)">
      Most stacks spread those three across source, runtime libraries, broker
      configuration and deployment manifests. Hale keeps them separate enough
      to reason about and joined enough for one checker to walk all three.
      The compiler will draw them. The communication graph of a four-locus program follows, rendered by <code>hale topology graph</code> from the build artifact.
    </p>
  </div>
  <figure class="graph">
    <div class="drawing" role="img" aria-label="Communication graph: Sampler publishes to the Readings topic, which Store and Monitor subscribe to; Monitor publishes to Alerts, which Pager subscribes to." set:html={busGraph} />
    <figcaption>
      <code>Readings</code> is one vertex with one edge in and two out: a
      single publish reaching two subscribers. The topic is a vertex because
      in Hale it is one, a declared entity with a wire subject and a payload
      contract. <code>Monitor</code> both subscribes and publishes, so the second hop is in the same picture.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Change one line so a value passes through a function-typed parameter,
      and the graph grows a vertex that is not part of the program.
    </p>
  </div>
  <figure class="graph">
    <div class="drawing" role="img" aria-label="Residue graph: the same program, with an unresolved indirect_call node attached to the apply function by a dashed edge." set:html={residueGraph} />
    <figcaption>
      The dashed box is a hole: a typed record of what the compiler could
      not determine, naming the relations it withdraws. Most program graphs
      show what was found. This one also shows where the compiler stopped
      looking, which is why a law that reaches that edge reports <em>uncertified</em>, a different answer from <em>holds</em>.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>A topic names the edge and says nothing about the mechanism</h2>
    <p>
      A topic is a typed, named edge. A publisher declares the authority to
      send; a subscriber declares the handler that receives. No channel
      handle or broker client is threaded through constructors.
    </p>
    <Hale code={bus} title="pipeline.hl" kind="service" />
    <p>
      A closed-world local edge may lower to a direct call. Other local edges
      use the bus. A binding can carry the same topic over a socket, shared
      memory, UDP, or an adapter you wrote.
    </p>

    <h2>main is the architectural linker</h2>
    <p>
      Domain loci say what the system is. <code>main</code> says where its
      parts run and how its topic edges travel. A test can omit bindings;
      production can split the same source across processes.
    </p>
    <Hale code={main} title="main.hl" kind="systems" />

    <h2>Effects include causality</h2>
    <p>
      Hale can constrain blocking, allocation, syscalls, time, entropy,
      publication, spawning and recursion across a reachable call path.
      Causality is an effect because it follows the same rule as the rest: a property is declared at one site
      and proven over everything that site can reach, and reaching does not
      stop where the call stack does. A function that publishes has handed
      its influence to whoever subscribes, and to whoever they publish to in
      turn. A declared dependency set says which subjects may arrive at the
      end of that chain, and the compiler walks it. <a href="/#causality">See it reject one</a>, or read <a href="/proof">the witness it hands back</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The law keeps going past the executable</h2>
    <p>
      Effects close around a function. Claims close around one assembled
      application. Neither can say anything about the entry point next door,
      or about the arrangement of binaries you actually deploy. The structure
      continues, and the same checker follows it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>constitution</strong> is a claimset written once and adopted
      per entry point. Every clause is still evaluated in the adopting
      main's own closed world: one text, many worlds, many independent
      verdicts. Composition is by union only, so a derived constitution can
      add a rule and cannot replace one. Which law applies is a property of
      where you deploy, so the binding lives in <code>hale.toml</code>, and <code>hale check --matrix</code> proves every entry point against every
      environment that runs it. An entry point listed in no environment is an
      error.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>fleet</strong> is one scale further out. Each application
      emits a byte-reproducible topology artifact. A plan names deployed
      instances and the routes between them. <code>hale fleet check</code> composes those artifacts, never merged source, and proves law no single
      binary can state. Matching topic declarations connect nothing; only an
      explicit route creates an edge, because an unbound topic is in-process
      by default and merging source would invent edges no deployment has.
      Signatures over the artifact's exact bytes, and per-instance binary
      digests, carry the certificate to the machine that runs it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A plan carries instances, routes, groups and claims, so it plays the
      role of a <code>main locus</code> one scale out, over separately
      compiled binaries. The plan composes certified artifacts and is
      itself JSON; Hale source has no fleet syntax. What recurs is the model.
    </p>

    <h2>Two axes</h2>
    <p>
      This page climbs one axis and the <a href="/docs">guide</a> climbs
      another. <strong>Scale</strong> is how large the composed system is,
      from function scope to fleet. <strong>Altitude</strong> is how much of
      the machinery you choose to control, from everyday code to explicit
      systems work. They are independent: one locus takes part at every
      scale while being written at whichever altitude the job needs, which is
      why the guide can teach a script before it teaches placement without
      leaving this model.
    </p>

    <h2>The commitments</h2>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><b>Nothing stateful is ownerless</b><p>State and lifetime have a structural home.</p></li>
      <li><b>No edge is ambient</b><p>Every communication edge is named, typed and authorized.</p></li>
      <li><b>Behaviour can be surfaced</b><p>Blocking, allocation, publication and dependence can become contracts.</p></li>
      <li><b>Failure goes somewhere structural</b><p>Recovery belongs to an owner with context.</p></li>
      <li><b>Deployment is part of the program</b><p>Placement and transport are declared in the program.</p></li>
      <li><b>Unknown is not proven</b><p>A hard guarantee fails closed at a certification boundary. Ordinary <code>hale check</code> still reports some topology findings as advisories; <code>hale verify</code>, a contract or a claim turns not knowing into a failure.</p></li>
      <li><b>Runtime change crosses declared seams</b><p>Dynamism moves between valid arrangements.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where it stands</h2>
    <p>Each stage hands evidence to the next.</p>
  </div>
  <StatusRail />
  <div class="prose">
    <p style="margin-top:var(--s8)" class="muted">
      That is the model you write. The one the compiler derives from it, the typed value behind claims, artifacts, fleet composition and lowering, is <a href="/docs/the-model">the semantic model</a>, which ends at the contract itself in <a href="https://github.com/hale-lang/hale/blob/main/spec/model.md"><code>spec/model.md</code></a>.
    </p>
  </div>
